-- title:
--   Lemma 11, p.21 — operations of one outer iteration
-- statement:
--   Assume the standing assumptions of §3: $\varepsilon>0$, $\gamma_0>0$, $z^0\in\mathrm{dom}\,B$, $0<\delta<1$, $0<\nu\le1/2$, $\eta\in[0,\nu/(1+\nu))$, $\rho_0\ge1$, $0<\tau_0\le1$, $\zeta>1$, $0<\sigma<1/\zeta$, Assumption 1 with $\mu=0$, and let $x^*$ be an arbitrary fixed solution of (1). Let $\Lambda=\rho_0\tau_0/(1-\sigma\zeta)$ and $L_{\mathcal Q},L_{\widehat{\mathcal Q}}$ be as in Lemma 2. For every completed outer iteration $k$ of Algorithm 2, the number of evaluations of $F$ and of the resolvent of $B$ in iteration $k$ is at most
--   $$
--   \bar M_k=6+2\left\lceil\frac{2\log\frac{(\bar r_0+\Lambda)(8+12\gamma_0L_{\mathcal Q})}{3\tau_k\sqrt{1-2\nu^2}\min\{L_{\widehat{\mathcal Q}}^{-1}\delta\xi,\gamma_0\}}}{\log\bigl(1+\frac{2}{\rho_k(1-\eta)}\min\{L_{\widehat{\mathcal Q}}^{-1}\delta\xi,\gamma_0\}\bigr)}\right\rceil_++\left\lceil\frac{\log\frac{\xi}{\gamma_0L_{\widehat{\mathcal Q}}}}{\log\delta}\right\rceil_+.
--   $$
--
--   Summing $\bar M_k$ over $k\le K$ gives the operation bound of Theorem 3.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: $F$ is a point-valued operator on $\mathrm{cl}(\mathrm{dom}\,B)$ → a total map on $\mathbb R^n$, constrained only on $\mathrm{cl}(\mathrm{dom}\,B)$; reason: values of $F$ elsewhere never enter $F+B$ or a run, since iterates lie in $\mathrm{dom}\,B$
--   - encoding: Assumption 1(d), the resolvent of $\gamma B$ can be exactly evaluated → a family $J$ with $J_\gamma=(I+\gamma B)^{-1}$ for every $\gamma>0$; reason: the assumption has no mathematical content; the family exists and is unique on $\gamma>0$
--   - encoding: iterates generated by Algorithm 2, $0\le k\in\mathbb K-1$ → the prefix predicate `Alg2.IsPrefix` at index $s$, with $k<s$; reason: $k\in\mathbb K-1$ says exactly that $z^{k+1}$ has been generated
--   - encoding: number of evaluations of $F$ and resolvent of $B$ → $\sum_t (n_t+1)$ over the iterations of the run, one number for both; reason: this is the count fixed by Remark 1(iii), p.5, and the proof of Theorem 2, p.19
--   - encoding: $\lceil\omega\rceil_+$ → `Nat.ceil`; reason: $(\lceil\omega\rceil)_+$ is the natural-number ceiling, for every real $\omega$
--   - generalization: $L_{\mathcal Q},L_{\widehat{\mathcal Q}}$ are the constants given by Lemma 2, for some choice → every pair valid in the sense of Lemma 2; reason: the proof uses only the two Lipschitz bounds, and Lemma 2 supplies such a pair, so the printed statement is the instance at that pair
-- source:
--   Lu & Mei, Primal-dual extrapolation methods for monotone inclusions under local Lipschitz continuity, arXiv:2206.00973v3, Lemma 11, p.21

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_Convergence_MonotoneOperators
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Setting
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Algorithm1
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Algorithm2

open InnerProductSpace ThreeOpSplitting.Convergence

namespace MonoIncExtrap

/-- **Lemma 11.** arXiv:2206.00973v3, Lemma 11, p.21. For a run of Algorithm 2 and a completed outer
iteration `k`, the number of evaluations of `F` and of the resolvent of `B` in iteration `k` is at
most `M̄_k` of (75).

**Formalization Note.** `c k` is the count `Σ(n_t + 1)` of the inner run, see `Alg2.InnerRun`.
`M̄_k` is built from any valid pair of Lemma 2. -/
theorem lemma_11 {n : ℕ}
    {B : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))}
    {I : Alg2.Input (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))} {xstar : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)}
    (hI : Alg2.Standing B I xstar)
    {z : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)} {c : ℕ → ℕ} {s : ℕ}
    (hz : Alg2.IsPrefix I z c s) {k : ℕ} (hk : k < s)
    {LQ LQhat : ℝ} (hv : Alg2.ValidQ B I xstar LQ LQhat) :
    c k ≤ I.MbarK (I.rbar xstar) LQ LQhat k := by sorry

end MonoIncExtrap
